[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 254, then narrowed to `char`, wrapping to -2. `printHexCharLine` takes a `char`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m which the variadic `printf` call promotes back to `int`, and `%02x`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reinterprets those 32 bits as `unsigned int` — so it prints `fffffffe`, not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `fe`, and the `02` minimum width has no effect. This is the original defect and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is reproduced deliberately.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The translation encodes this as `char_hex as i32` then `as u32` for formatting.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Printing the byte instead (`char_hex as u8`) yields `fe` and was confirmed to be
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m caught by the suite.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Dead branches in the C (no input can reach them)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These were identified by reading the source; they are unreachable rather than
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m untested, and are pinned by `golden_outputs_match_c`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `printLine`'s `line != NULL` guard — the only call site passes a string
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   literal, so the NULL path never runs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `if (data > 0)` in `bad()`, `goodG2B()` and `goodB2G()` — `data` is a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   compile-time constant (127, 2, 127) in each, so the guard is always true.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - The `if (data < (CHAR_MAX/2))` *then* branch in `goodB2G()` — `data` is 127
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and `CHAR_MAX/2` is 63 (integer division), so only the `else` ever runs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `goodB2G` therefore never prints a hex value at all.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `goodB2G`'s `data = ' '` is dead: it is overwritten by `data = CHAR_MAX` on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the next line. The Rust keeps the dead store behind `#[allow(unused_assignments)]`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## The only input-dependent behaviour: `scanf("%d")`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Because every other branch is constant-folded, the entire input space collapses
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m onto how `%d` converts. That is where a translation can realistically diverge,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m so it was tested exhaustively. Confirmed identical:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **No conversion leaves `x` untouched.** Empty input, whitespace-only input, a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   lone `-` or `+`, `- 5`, `--5`, `+-5`, `.5`, letters, a leading NUL, a leading
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `0xff`, and a UTF-8 BOM all fail to convert, so `x` keeps its initialiser of 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and the program takes `bad()`. The ignored return value is what makes this
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   observable.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Whitespace skipping** covers all six C-locale space characters, including
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   vertical tab (`0x0b`) and form feed (`0x0c`), and reads across newlines.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Conversion stops at the first non-digit**, so `0x10` converts as just `0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (→ `bad()`) while `010` converts as decimal 10 (→ `good()`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Saturate at `long`, then truncate to `int`.** The C library converts at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `long` width, clamps to `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` on overflow, and the store into
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `int` truncates. This produces branch flips that a naive translation gets
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   wrong:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | input | long value | truncated `int` | branch |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   |---|---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | `4294967295` | 4294967295 | -1 | `good()` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | `4294967296` | 4294967296 | **0** | `bad()` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | `9223372036854775807` | `LONG_MAX` | -1 | `good()` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | `-9223372036854775808` | `LONG_MIN` | **0** | `bad()` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | `99999999999999999999` | saturates to `LONG_MAX` | -1 | `good()` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   | `-99999999999999999999` | saturates to `LONG_MIN` | **0** | `bad()` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   A translation that saturated into `int` instead of truncating would send
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `4294967296` to `good()`; that defect was confirmed to be caught.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Digit runs beyond any integer width** — up to 10,000 nines, in both signs,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   plus long leading-zero runs — saturate identically.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
