The key structural finding is that almost every branch in the C is unreachable. [38;5;10mdata[0m is a compile-time constant in [38;5;10mbad()[0m, [38;5;10mgoodG2B()[0m and [38;5;10mgoodB2G()[0m, so all three [38;5;10mif (data > 0)[0m guards are always true, the [38;5;10mdata < CHAR_MAX/2[0m then-branch never runs (127 vs 63), and [38;5;10mprintLine[0m's NULL guard has no NULL call site. That leaves exactly two possible outputs, and the whole input space collapses onto how [38;5;10mscanf("%d")[0m converts — so that is where I concentrated.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
That conversion is where a translation would realistically break, and it is subtler than it looks. [38;5;10m%d[0m converts at [38;5;10mlong[0m width, saturates at [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m, then [3mtruncates[23m into [38;5;10mint[0m, which flips the branch at non-obvious points: [38;5;10m4294967295[0m → -1 → [38;5;10mgood()[0m, but [38;5;10m4294967296[0m → 0 → [38;5;10mbad()[0m; [38;5;10m-9223372036854775808[0m → 0 → [38;5;10mbad()[0m. Since [38;5;10mmain[0m ignores scanf's return value, every matching failure is also observable as [38;5;10mbad()[0m via [38;5;10mx[0m keeping its initialiser. The existing Rust already models all of this, and matched on a power-of-two boundary sweep to 2^71, digit runs up to 10,000 nines, and two seeded fuzzers.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mSuite validity.[22m Rather than trust green tests, I injected defects. Four were killed: dropping sign extension in [38;5;10m%02x[0m (prints [38;5;10mfe[0m instead of [38;5;10mfffffffe[0m), [38;5;10mif (x > 0)[0m for [38;5;10mif (x != 0)[0m, a flipped [38;5;10mgoodB2G[0m comparison, and saturating instead of truncating the store into [38;5;10mint[0m. One survived and should — removing the scanner's one-byte pushback is unobservable because nothing reads stdin after the single [38;5;10mscanf[0m. That is an equivalent mutant, not a gap.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
