One caveat worth stating: the [38;5;10m%d[0m overflow semantics I verified against are glibc's on x86-64 Linux, where [38;5;10mchar[0m is signed and [38;5;10mlong[0m is 64-bit. Both the [38;5;10mfffffffe[0m output and the truncation table depend on all three facts, so this equivalence is platform-specific by construction — as it must be, since the C's behaviour is too. [38;5;10mtranslation/ERRORS.md[0m records the dead branches, the truncation table, the mutation results, and one false-pass bug in my own throwaway shell harness (leading [38;5;10m-[0m inputs were being eaten by [38;5;10mprintf[0m as options, silently re-testing stale bytes) that briefly made seven negative-number cases look verified when they weren't.[0m[0m
